# ce-chatgpt-bulk-delete repository instructions for Codex

## Repository scope

This repository is our maintained version of the Chrome extension
`ce-chatgpt-bulk-delete`.

The extension manages ChatGPT conversation history. Changes can affect
selection, classification, persistence, rate limiting, and destructive
conversation-management actions, so preserve safety boundaries carefully.

Current task goals belong in the active Codex task/chat. Durable development
workflow belongs in maintained repository documentation once established.

Before substantial work:

- inspect current Git state;
- verify the active worktree, branch, and HEAD;
- inspect relevant source/tests/docs before editing;
- preserve unrelated user changes;
- distinguish upstream behavior from our maintained modifications.

## Active worktree

The active repository for this workspace is:

    __CE_CHATGPT_BULK_DELETE_WORKTREE__

Do not edit the sibling canonical checkout:

    /home/didi/research/dev/tools/ce-chatgpt-bulk-delete

unless an explicit task authorizes work there.

Do not edit other sibling worktrees.

## Project architecture

Current upstream source is a small Chrome extension centered around:

- `manifest.json`
- `background.js`
- `content.js`
- `dashboard.html`
- `dashboard.css`
- `dashboard.js`
- `db.js`
- `README.md`
- `ARCHITECTURE.md`

Inspect current source rather than assuming these files or their responsibilities
remain unchanged.

## Engineering rules

- Preserve existing behavior unless the active task explicitly changes it.
- Prefer small explicit boundaries over unrelated refactors.
- Treat destructive conversation actions conservatively.
- Never interpret an unknown/unclassified conversation as safe for deletion
  merely for convenience.
- Never represent a failed remote deletion as a successful local deletion.
- Keep local persistence consistent with confirmed remote outcomes.
- Treat ChatGPT internal endpoints as unsupported/changeable implementation
  details; inspect observed current behavior when endpoint/schema details matter.
- Do not expose ChatGPT authorization tokens, cookies, credentials, or private
  conversation contents in logs, fixtures, commits, or reports.
- Do not weaken safety behavior merely to make a test pass.
- When changing persisted IndexedDB data, consider compatibility with records
  created by previous extension versions.
- Keep extension permissions minimal; do not broaden permissions without a
  task-specific reason.

## Testing and gates

There is currently no assumption of a large universal test suite.

For each task:

1. determine the narrowest relevant verification from current source;
2. add focused automated tests when practical and justified;
3. perform syntax/static checks appropriate to touched JavaScript/JSON/shell;
4. run `git diff --check`;
5. use a small real Chrome smoke when browser/runtime integration changed.

Do not mass-delete real conversations as a test.

Destructive browser smokes must use only a very small number of explicitly
disposable conversations selected by the user.

## Git and worktree safety

- Begin substantial work with:
  - `pwd`
  - `git branch --show-current`
  - `git rev-parse HEAD`
  - `git status --short --branch`
- Preserve unrelated changes.
- Do not use `git reset --hard`, `git clean`, or broad restore operations.
- Do not push, merge, force-push, delete branches/worktrees, or rewrite shared
  history unless explicitly authorized.
- For a normal substantial increment, stop dirty/uncommitted for independent
  review unless the authoritative task explicitly authorizes checkpoint commits.
- Local `AGENTS.md` and `.codex/*` files are worktree support files and must not
  enter feature commits unless a later task deliberately promotes generic
  templates/bootstrap infrastructure into tracked project files.

## External/network actions

Routine repository-local inspection/editing/testing is allowed within the
configured workspace.

Do not perform external destructive actions, publish releases, push Git state,
or operate on the user's ChatGPT account unless the active task explicitly
authorizes that operation.

Browser/runtime testing involving the user's actual ChatGPT account is normally
user-run.

## Documentation

When maintained behavior changes, update the relevant maintained documentation
in the same increment when appropriate.

Do not put temporary campaign/task state into repository-wide instructions.

## Completion report

For meaningful tasks report:

- files changed;
- exact behavior changed;
- tests/checks run and outcomes;
- unresolved uncertainty;
- browser/manual verification still required;
- current Git status;
- whether any commit occurred.

Do not claim push/merge unless it actually occurred.

## Autonomous multi-increment work

For an explicitly approved multi-increment task, read:

    .codex/CODEX_AUTONOMOUS_EXECUTION_POLICY.txt

and use:

    .codex/CODEX_MASTER_INCREMENT_TEMPLATE.txt

Do not infer autonomous commit permission unless the active task explicitly
grants it.
